WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.940
Secure input handling in C++ requires explicit validation of trust boundaries, parsing results, lifetimes, ranges, indexes, and resource use.

2
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:18.680
C++ gives you tools that make safe designs easier, but those tools still need clear rules.

3
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:29.020
std::string owns data, string_view borrows it, containers track size, RAII improves cleanup, and modern parsing APIs can report detailed status.

4
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.800
None of that helps if the program ignores where input came from or how a value will be used.

5
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.000
Strong C++ programs validate before use.

6
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.780
They check stream state and parsing results.

7
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.280
They preserve lengths with data.

8
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.900
They verify numeric ranges before narrowing, indexing, allocating, or moving through buffers.

9
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.200
They enforce resource limits before growing containers.

10
00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:01.980
They treat file paths, serialized data, structured formats, and binary records as untrusted until policy and format checks pass.

11
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:07.200
They use safer abstractions while still checking the assumptions those abstractions cannot know.

12
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:15.720
The practical goal is predictable behavior under malformed, oversized, incomplete, hostile, or unexpected input.

13
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:27.660
Good input handling rejects unsafe data cleanly, cleans up reliably, avoids leaking sensitive details, and leaves validated values in a form later code can use safely.

14
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:39.600
When teams test boundary cases, fuzz parsers, review trust boundaries, and use warnings and sanitizers, secure input handling becomes a normal engineering habit rather than a last-minute patch.
